        q: `A 6-year-old male child presents with a history of recurrent infections with pyogenic bacteria (e.g., <i>Staphylococcus aureus</i> and <i>Streptococcus pneumoniae</i>). After infections, there is an expected increase in the total white blood cell (WBC) count, of which 90% are neutrophils. However, histologic examination of tissues reveals very few neutrophils. An analysis of the patient’s neutrophil function through in vivo live cell imaging of venules shows a defect in rolling. A defect or deficiency affecting which of the following molecules is likely to be responsible for the increased susceptibility to infection in this patient?`,
