        q: `A 12-month-old male child with a 6-month history of repeated infections presents with pneumonia and with elevated numbers of neutrophils in the peripheral blood. Aspiration of the lung abscess shows neutrophils. Laboratory studies show that his neutrophils phagocytose and kill bacteria normally in the presence of normal human serum (but not the patient’s serum). They migrate normally in a chemotaxis assay. Serum complement levels are normal. Which of the following is the most likely cause for the increased susceptibility to infection?`,
