        optionFeedback: [`Colloid osmotic pressure changes mainly because of decreased production of albumin (as in liver disease) or increased loss of albumin (as in kidney disease). Decreased colloid osmotic pressure leads to transudation of fluid and edema in multiple tissues.`,`Hydrostatic pressure of the blood increases in conditions of compromised venous return, as in congestive heart failure. The increased pressure also leads to transudation of plasma and edema formation. This may have happened in this patient if his disease was congestive heart failure, not complicated by pneumonia.`,`Lymphatic pressure increases in conditions of blocked lymphatic drainage, as in metastatic tumors in lymph nodes. This may lead to lymphedema.`,`Reduced renal perfusion activates the renin-angiotensin system, leading to sodium retention and increased blood pressure. This does not cause edema. `,`The clinical history of fever and worsening pulmonary function, and the x-ray, are indicative of acute pneumonia. The pleural fluid has a high specific gravity (normal ~1.00), indicating leakage of plasma proteins, and contains neutrophils. These are typical of exudates, which result from increased vascular permeability as in acute inflammation.`],
