        optionFeedback: [`Leukocytes do attach to endothelial cells as a prelude to their migration through vessels to sites of inflammation, but this reaction is mediated mainly by cytokines, not by ROS.`,`The respiratory burst is an NADPH-dependent oxidation reaction in phagolysosomes that leads to the production of reactive oxygen species (ROS), a type of free radical that destroys ingested microbes. ROS that are released into the extracellular environment also kill microbes outside the neutrophils. `,`Leukocyte production in the bone marrow increases during inflammation to meet the increased demand and replace cells that die after they enter inflammatory sites. This increased marrow output is mediated by cytokines, mainly colony-stimulating factors (CSFs). `,`Bacteria are opsonized by antibodies, complement products (mainly C3b and C4b), and plasma proteins and are phagocytosed via leukocyte plasma membrane receptors, including Fc and complement receptors and other receptors such as lectins. ROS are not involved in opsonization and phagocytosis, but they kill microbes once these have been phagocytosed.`,`Bacteria are opsonized by antibodies, complement products (mainly C3b and C4b), and plasma proteins and are phagocytosed via leukocyte plasma membrane receptors, including Fc and complement receptors and other receptors such as lectins. ROS are not involved in opsonization and phagocytosis, but they kill microbes once these have been phagocytosed.`],
